    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::train_step">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::train_step</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs a single A2C training update on a batch. The method accepts numpy arrays for observations, actions, discounted returns and advantage estimates. It first concatenates actions and advantages column-wise into a (batch_size, 2) array (actions[:, None] and advantages[:, None]) to form the input expected by the policy loss, then calls self.model.train_on_batch with observations as input and [actions_and_advantages, returns] as the two targets. It returns a dict of floating-point training metrics extracted from the model's returned losses: 'total_loss' (losses[0]), 'policy_loss' (losses[1]) and 'value_loss' (losses[2]). The implementation assumes the model is configured to accept these targets (policy head that uses action+advantage and a value head that regresses to returns).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::_policy_loss">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::_policy_loss</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Computes the A2C policy loss with entropy regularization. The function expects a single tensor actions_and_advantages containing actions and their corresponding advantage weights concatenated along the last axis; it splits this into actions (first half) and advantages (second half), casting actions to int32. It computes the policy loss as a weighted sparse categorical cross-entropy (from_logits=True) using advantages as sample weights, and computes the policy entropy by evaluating categorical_crossentropy(logits, logits, from_logits=True) (which yields -sum p log p). The final returned tensor is policy_loss - entropy_coeff * entropy_loss (entropy is subtracted so the optimizer, which minimizes, effectively maximizes entropy).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::save_model">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::save_model</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of A2CAgent that saves the agent's neural network weights to disk. It calls self.model.save_weights(filepath) using the provided filepath (string) and writes an info-level log via self.logger indicating where the model weights were saved. Note: it saves weights only, not the full model object or architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::ProbabilityDistribution::call">
      <data key="d0">agent::ProbabilityDistribution::call</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Given a tensor of class logits, this method samples one categorical action per row by drawing from the categorical distribution defined by the logits. It calls tf.random.categorical(logits, 1) to produce one sampled index per input row and tf.squeeze(..., axis=-1) to remove the singleton sample dimension, returning an int64 tensor of sampled class indices (shape typically [batch_size] or a scalar for a single example).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CModel::__init__">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CModel::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the A2CModel that builds and initializes the neural network components used by an Advantage Actor-Critic agent. It accepts the number of discrete actions (num_actions) and a configuration dictionary (config), calls the parent initializer with the name 'a2c_model', and stores the inputs. The constructor creates two convolutional feature-extraction layers (keras.layers.Convolution2D) and a Flatten layer, then defines separate hidden Dense layers for the actor and critic paths using sizes from config['hidden_size']. It adds a value head (Dense(1)) that predicts a scalar state value and a policy logits head (Dense(num_actions)) that produces unnormalized action logits. It also instantiates a ProbabilityDistribution helper for sampling actions and sets up a module logger. The config keys referenced include 'filter_no', 'kernel_size', 'strides', and 'hidden_size', and ReLU activations are used for intermediate layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CModel::call">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CModel::call</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs the forward pass of an A2C actor-critic model. It accepts an input tensor (expected shape [batch_size, channels, height, width]), converts it to tf.float32, runs it through two convolutional layers and a flattening layer, then splits into separate actor and critic paths. The actor path passes through actor_hidden then policy_head to produce policy logits; the critic path passes through critic_hidden then value_head to produce value estimates. Returns a tuple (policy_logits, value_estimates). Note: policy_logits are raw logits (no softmax applied) and value_estimates are the critic outputs (typically a per-batch scalar). The method relies on the instance layers: conv1, conv2, flatten, actor_hidden, critic_hidden, policy_head, and value_head.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::__init__">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the A2CAgent class. It stores the provided configuration, creates a logger, reads A2C hyperparameters (gamma, entropy coefficient, value coefficient, learning rate) from the config dictionary, instantiates an A2CModel using the environment action dimension and model config, compiles that model with an RMSprop optimizer and two custom losses (policy and value: self._policy_loss and self._value_loss), and logs that the agent has been initialized. The method initializes the following instance attributes: config, logger, gamma, entropy_coeff, value_coeff, learning_rate, and model.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CModel::action_value">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CModel::action_value</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">For a single environment observation, this method returns a sampled action and the critic value estimate. If the input is a 3-D observation (no batch), it first adds a batch dimension. It then calls self.predict(observation) to obtain policy logits and the value estimate, uses self.dist.predict(logits) to produce an action from the logits, and finally squeezes the trailing singleton dimension(s) from both the action and value outputs before returning (so callers get scalar or 1-D outputs rather than batch-wrapped arrays).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::get_action">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::get_action</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">A method on A2CAgent that takes a single environment observation (numpy array), asks the agent's model for an action and a value estimate via self.model.action_value(observation), and returns them as a tuple (action as int, value estimate as float). It simply forwards the observation to the model and casts the returned action and value to the specified primitive types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::compute_returns_and_advantages">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::compute_returns_and_advantages</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">compute_returns_and_advantages computes discounted episode returns and simple advantages for a trajectory. Given arrays of rewards, value estimates, and done flags, and an optional next_value for bootstrapping, it builds the returns by backward recursion using returns[t] = rewards[t] + gamma * returns[t+1] * (1 - dones[t]) with the last-step initialized as rewards[-1] + gamma * next_value * (1 - dones[-1]). Advantages are then computed as returns - values. Both outputs are returned as float32 NumPy arrays. This implementation uses the agent's self.gamma and does not implement GAE (it is a plain return-minus-baseline advantage).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::_value_loss">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::_value_loss</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Private method of A2CAgent that computes the value (critic) loss. It calculates the mean squared error between the target returns and the predicted values using kls.mean_squared_error, then scales that MSE by the agent's value_coeff hyperparameter. The result is a scalar/tensor loss used to train the value network.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::load_model">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::load_model</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the A2CAgent class that loads saved weights into the agent's neural network. It calls self.model.load_weights(filepath) to restore weights from the given file path and logs an informational message via self.logger. The method does not return a value and will raise an error if the file is missing or incompatible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::A2CAgent::get_model_summary">
      <data key="d0">agent::A2CAgent::get_model_summary</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">A simple wrapper method on A2CAgent that forwards to the underlying model's summary() call. It calls and returns self.model.summary(), which prints the model architecture to stdout (and typically returns None for standard Keras/TensorFlow models) — no parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acd9afa9c91c00d207b7d3d68ec7f1f6</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">A2CTrainer is a training manager for an Advantage Actor‑Critic (A2C) agent. It initializes logging, a CcoreFemmEnv environment and an A2CAgent from a provided configuration, and exposes a train() method that runs the main training loop until configured episode or update limits are reached. During training it repeatedly collects fixed-size experience batches (_collect_batch), computes returns and advantages and performs agent updates (_train_agent), evaluates the agent periodically in a separate evaluation environment (_evaluate_agent), and saves model checkpoints and final training artifacts. It records metrics (episode rewards/lengths, per‑update training metrics, evaluation results), compiles and JSON‑serializes results (converting numpy types) to training_results.json, and writes logs and render outputs to the results directory. Utility methods include checkpoint saving (_save_checkpoint), result compilation (_compile_results), serialization helper (_make_serializable), and close() to release the environment. Configurable behavior is driven by entries such as max_episodes, max_updates, batch_size, play_interval and save_frequency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::unknown">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">I cannot access the implementation from the provided snippet (the file shows only the class header "class A2CTrainer:" and the actual code is unavailable), so I cannot verbatim describe its lines. Based on the class name and standard A2C trainer implementations, this class likely encapsulates the training logic for an Advantage Actor–Critic algorithm. Typical responsibilities you can expect are: initializing policy and value networks (or a combined actor–critic), optimizers and hyperparameters (learning rate, entropy coefficient, value loss coefficient, gradient clipping); interacting with (possibly vectorized) environments to collect rollouts of fixed length; computing discounted returns and advantages (optionally using GAE), forming the actor (policy) loss, critic (value) loss and entropy bonus, summing them to a total loss, and performing backward/optimizer steps; handling bootstrapped value targets for incomplete episodes; scheduling/logging training metrics (losses, rewards, episode lengths), checkpoint saving and model loading, and moving tensors to the correct device (CPU/GPU). Without the source, I cannot confirm which of these features are implemented or any parameter names/APIs used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_train_agent">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_train_agent</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">_Trains the A2C agent on a single collected experience batch and returns training metrics._ Given a batch_data dictionary (containing 'observations', 'actions', 'rewards', 'values', 'dones', and 'next_value'), the method: 1) computes discounted returns and advantages by calling self.agent.compute_returns_and_advantages with rewards, values, dones and next_value; 2) performs a training step via self.agent.train_step using observations, actions, the computed returns and advantages and receives training metrics; 3) augments the returned metrics with summary statistics (mean_reward, mean_value, mean_advantage) and the batch_size (len(observations)); and 4) returns the combined metrics dictionary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Runs the main A2C training loop for the trainer instance. It initializes the environment and then repeatedly collects experience batches via _collect_batch, trains the agent on non-empty batches via _train_agent (incrementing update_count), and tracks per-update training metrics. It increments episode_count when a batch indicates the episode finished, records episode rewards and lengths, resets the environment for new episodes, and otherwise appends the next observation to obs_history. Periodically (every play_interval updates) it runs _evaluate_agent and stores evaluation results, and it saves model checkpoints every save_frequency episodes via _save_checkpoint. The loop exits when either max_episodes or max_updates is reached. After finishing it performs a final evaluation, compiles and saves results with _compile_results and _save_results, logs timing information, and returns the results dictionary. The method uses instance lists (training_metrics, evaluation_results, episode_rewards, episode_lengths) and the logger for progress reporting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_save_checkpoint">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_save_checkpoint</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates a checkpoint file for the trainer's agent by saving the agent model to disk. It ensures a 'checkpoints' folder exists under self.results_dir, builds a filename of the form 'model_ep{episode}_up{update}.h5', calls self.agent.save_model(...) with that path, and logs an informational message. This method has no return value and has side effects on the filesystem and the trainer's logger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_save_results">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_save_results</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Private method that writes a training-results dictionary to disk as JSON. It builds the path results_dir/'training_results.json', converts any non-JSON-native objects (e.g., numpy arrays) to serializable forms via self._make_serializable, dumps the resulting structure to the file with indent=2, and logs an info message indicating where the results were saved. Uses self.results_dir and self.logger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the A2CTrainer class. It stores the provided configuration and results directory (converting to a pathlib.Path and creating the directory), configures Python logging (file handler to results_dir/training.log and a stream handler, using log level from config), creates a logger, initializes the environment (CcoreFemmEnv) with parameters taken from the config including a render path inside the results directory, instantiates the A2C agent with the same config, reads and stores training parameters from the config (max_episodes, max_updates, batch_size, play_interval, save_frequency), initializes empty lists for tracking episode rewards, episode lengths, training metrics, and evaluation results, and logs an info message indicating initialization completed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_evaluate_agent">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_evaluate_agent</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Runs a single evaluation episode for the current agent and returns summary metrics. The method constructs a new CcoreFemmEnv using parameters from self.config (max_steps, env_dim, femm_path) and a render output directory under self.results_dir, sets the env logger to WARNING, and resets the environment. It then steps through the episode until done, choosing actions with self.agent.get_action(obs), accumulating total reward and step count, and optionally rendering each step if config['logging']['render_evaluation'] is true. After closing the environment it returns a dictionary with keys: 'reward' (total episode reward), 'length' (number of steps), and 'net_force' and 'iron_count' taken from the final step info (defaulting to 0.0 and 0 if absent).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_collect_batch">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_collect_batch</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Collects a batch of experience from the environment starting at initial_obs until either self.batch_size transitions have been gathered or the episode terminates. At each step it queries self.agent.get_action(obs) to obtain an action and the agent’s value estimate for the current observation, records the observation, action, value, reward and done flag, steps the environment, and stops if done. After collection it obtains a next_value estimate for the final observation (or 0.0 if the episode ended). It returns a dictionary containing numpy arrays for observations, actions, rewards, values and dones, plus next_value, next_obs, a boolean episode_finished flag, and an iron_count extracted from the last env.info (or 0 if no steps were collected).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_compile_results">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_compile_results</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Builds and returns a summary dictionary of a completed A2C training run. Given training_time, episodes and updates counts, it collects instance attributes (self.episode_rewards, self.episode_lengths, self.training_metrics, self.evaluation_results, self.config) and packages them with the supplied counts. It also computes a final_performance sub-dictionary that contains: mean_reward_last_10 (mean of the last 10 rewards or the mean of all rewards if fewer than 10), mean_length_last_10 (same logic for episode lengths), best_reward (maximum episode reward or 0.0 if none), and final_evaluation (the last evaluation result or an empty dict if none). Means are computed using numpy.mean. The function returns this data as a Dict[str, Any].</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::_make_serializable">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::_make_serializable</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Instance method that converts Python objects containing NumPy types into JSON-serializable native Python types. It handles np.ndarray by converting to lists via tolist(), np.integer and np.floating by casting to int and float, respectively, and recursively walks dicts and lists to convert their contents. Any other objects are returned unchanged. The method is intended to prepare nested structures for JSON serialization, though it does not special-case tuples, sets or other custom types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::A2CTrainer::close">
      <data key="d0">trainer::A2CTrainer::close</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of A2CTrainer that performs cleanup: it calls self.env.close() to release the environment resources and logs an informational message "Trainer closed". It takes no parameters and returns None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-277b3f6ab00ea1af8f0bf65c82548ac2</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">CcoreFemmEnv is a custom reinforcement-learning environment that lets an agent iteratively place magnetic material inside a discretized C‑core actuator design space and evaluates electromagnetic performance using the FEMM finite‑element tool. The environment maintains a 2D grid state (with boundary, air, copper coil, iron, and pointer IDs), a movable pointer that performs worm‑like placement (3×3 iron block) via discrete actions (directional moves), and enforces collisions/ bounds with penalties. It builds and updates a base FEMM model (coils, armature, boundaries), writes changed material blocks into the FEMM document, runs FEMM analyses, and computes a reward from the magnetic field in the armature (average B magnitude ×1000) while also extracting net force and B‑field distributions for diagnostics. Episodes are managed with reset/step semantics, history tracking (states, actions, rewards, forces, B‑fields), termination on max steps or exceeding iron count, optional data augmentation via random horizontal flip, and rendering/saving of state images. The observation returned to the agent is a local design window concatenated with the corresponding B‑field distribution (shape given by STATE_SIZE). The class exposes action_space and observation_space properties and relies on external constants (ACTIONS, MATERIAL_IDS, GEOMETRY, PENALTY, etc.) and an installed FEMM API.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::unknown">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">No implementation was available for this entity in the provided source excerpt. Based on its name and its location in a DRL/topology-optimization project (Chp5_DRL_TO/src/femm_environment/environment.py), CcoreFemmEnv appears to be a class that implements an environment wrapper around a FEMM (Finite Element Method Magnetics or similar FEM tool) simulation for use with reinforcement learning or optimization. Responsibilities likely include: initializing and configuring the FEMM simulation (geometry, materials, mesh and solver settings), exposing standard environment methods such as reset(), step(action), render(), and close(), maintaining observation and action spaces, translating RL actions into geometry or simulation parameter changes, running simulations to compute physical quantities (fields, forces, energies), and computing rewards/termination conditions. If you need an exact description of attributes and method behavior, please provide the actual source lines for this class or the full file so I can extract precise implementation details.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::action_space">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::action_space</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Instance method that returns the environment's action-space size as an integer. It is a simple getter with no parameters or side effects and returns the constant ACTION_DIM.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_coils">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_coils</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates two rectangular electromagnetic coil regions in the FEMM model using geometry parameters stored on self.geometry. For the left coil it clears any existing labels/nodes/segments in the target area, draws a rectangle spanning x=[0, coil_length] and y=[coil_start, coil_start+coil_width], places a block label at the rectangle center and assigns a Copper block property with an "icoil" current of -500. For the right coil it computes a mirrored vertical position (coil2_start = 16 - (coil_start + coil_width)), similarly clears the area, draws the rectangle, adds a block label and sets a Copper block property with an "icoil" current of +500. Overall this function sets up two oppositely driven coil blocks (using the specified geometry and currents) in the FEMM environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_check_termination">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_check_termination</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of CcoreFemmEnv that determines whether the current episode should terminate. It takes the current state (a numpy array of material IDs), counts how many entries equal MATERIAL_IDS['iron'], and returns True if that iron count exceeds MAX_IRON. It also returns True if the environment's step_count has reached or exceeded self.max_steps. Otherwise it returns False.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::__init__">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the C-core FEMM environment that initializes and configures the environment instance. It accepts optional parameters for maximum episode steps, environment dimensions, start pointer position, FEMM installation path, render output path, and logging level. The method stores these parameters, creates the render directory if needed, and configures Python logging. It initializes episode and step tracking variables (frame_idx, step_count, done) and histories for state, action, reward, force, and magnetic-field values. Position and movement are set from the provided start position and a fixed worm_step_size. Several physics-related state arrays are allocated: bfield_state (length 15), bfield_xy (shape based on env_dim and GEOMETRY['buffer']), and mu_state (same shape as bfield_xy), and net_force is initialized. It also sets data-augmentation flags (flip and flip_actions), copies geometry parameters from GEOMETRY, and leaves the FEMM document handle (femm_doc) unset. The constructor relies on module-level defaults/constants such as MAX_STEPS, ENV_DIM, FEMM_PATH, MODEL_PATH, GEOMETRY, and ACTION_FLIP and logs that initialization completed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_armature">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_armature</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates the magnetic armature region in the FEMM model using the instance's geometry parameters. The method first selects a slightly inset rectangle covering the armature area and deletes any existing labels, nodes, and segments inside it to clear the region. It then draws a new rectangle spanning x = 0 to arm_length and y = arm_start to arm_start + arm_width, adds a block label at the rectangle's center, and assigns that block the material and block properties ('Cold rolled low carbon strip steel' plus the provided meshing/property flags). The routine relies on the object's geometry keys: arm_start, arm_length, and arm_width.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_get_observation">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_get_observation</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Takes the full state array and builds the agent observation: it extracts a 15-row by 22-column design window from state using self.geometry['buffer'] for the row start and columns 5:27. It also extracts the matching slice from self.bfield_dist. If self.flip == 1 it performs a reflection padding along the row axis (adds 21 reflected rows), then concatenates the padded state window and padded bfield slice as two channels and finally selects the first 15 rows and last 22 columns. If flip != 1 it simply stacks the state window and bfield slice. The function returns a NumPy array of shape (2, 15, 22) where channel 0 is the state window and channel 1 is the bfield distribution slice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_boundaries">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_boundaries</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Initializes the FEMM model’s geometric and boundary setup. Specifically, it draws an outer rectangular domain (0,100)–(100,-100), places a block label at (50,0) and assigns the ‘Air’ block property. It then defines a Neumann boundary property, selects the outer rectangle’s segments (via a slightly enlarged selection rectangle) and applies the ‘neumann’ segment property (with a marker/index set in the call). Next it selects three specific segments by coordinate (95,-95), (55,95) and (55,-95), creates a Dirichlet boundary property, and applies the ‘dirichlet’ segment property to those selected segments. The method performs these FEMM API calls to configure boundary conditions and returns None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_update_femm_materials">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_update_femm_materials</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Updates FEMM (Finite Element Method Magnetics) block materials to reflect changes in the environment state array. The method compares the provided state to the previous state (or, if no history exists, finds all cells equal to MATERIAL_IDS['iron']), determines which grid positions changed, and for each changed cell that is set to the iron material it: selects a label at the cell coordinates offset by +0.4, deletes any selected labels, adds a new block label at that offset, and sets the block material properties to 'Cold rolled low carbon strip steel' (using femm.mi_setblockprop with parameters 0,0,'&amp;lt;None&amp;gt;',0,3,0). The function relies on self.state_history and the MATERIAL_IDS['iron'] constant and only handles setting iron-material blocks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_save_frame">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_save_frame</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Saves the most recent environment state as an image file. If state_history is empty it returns immediately. Otherwise it takes the last state array, extracts a fixed subregion (rows geometry['buffer'] to geometry['buffer']+15 and columns 5 to 26), normalizes that subarray by its maximum value, ensures a directory render_path/Eps_{frame_idx} exists, constructs a filename containing frame_idx, the provided step and description, and the last reward (formatted to two decimals), converts the image to unsigned integer format and writes it to disk with imageio. The method returns None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::reset">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::reset</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Resets the simulated FEMM environment to the start of a new episode and returns the initial observation (np.ndarray). Specifically, it increments the episode/frame counter, clears episode flags and histories (states, actions, rewards, forces, magnetic field records), resets agent position (posR, posC) and worm_step_size, zeros physics-related state (net_force, bfield_state, bfield_xy, mu_state), and picks a random flip bit for data augmentation. It then rebuilds the geometry (_create_geometry), creates the initial environment state (_create_initial_state), stores that state and a default initial action (4), computes and stores the initial reward, saves the first frame (index 0, tag "reset"), logs the reset, and finally returns the processed observation produced by _get_observation(initial_state).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_initial_state">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_initial_state</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates and returns a 2D NumPy array representing the initial material layout (design space) for the FEMM environment. It initializes the whole grid to the boundary material id, carves out a rectangular design window set to air, places two copper coil regions (one at coil_start and a second mirrored coil computed by coil2_start), and places an iron armature rectangle. Dimensions and positions are read from self.geometry (buffer, window_length, window_width, coil_start, coil_width, coil_length, arm_start, arm_width). The resulting array contains integer material IDs (e.g., boundary, air, copper, iron) for each cell and is returned.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_calculate_bfield_distribution">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_calculate_bfield_distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Samples the magnetic flux density from FEMM and stores three derived representations used by the environment state. Specifically, it: (1) fills self.bfield_state (length 15) with the magnitude sqrt(Bx^2+By^2) sampled at 15 armature points located at (row+0.5, 26.5); (2) populates self.bfield_xy, a 2D grid over the design region, with the magnitude sqrt(Bx^2+By^2) sampled at each cell center (r+0.5, c+0.5); and (3) computes self.bfield_dist as the row-wise sum of squared magnitudes across self.bfield_xy (rounded to two decimals). It calls femm.mo_getb to obtain B components and has no return value (it updates the instance attributes).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::render">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::render</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Renders the environment by saving a frame of the current state. If state_history is empty the method does nothing; otherwise it calls the internal _save_frame(self.step_count, 'render'). The optional mode parameter is accepted but unused and the method returns None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::observation_space">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::observation_space</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of environment::CcoreFemmEnv that returns the shape of the observation space. It is a simple accessor that returns the constant STATE_SIZE (a Tuple[int, ...]) describing the dimensionality/shape of the environment observations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::step">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::step</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs one timestep of the CcoreFemmEnv environment. Given an integer action (docstring suggests 0–7), it (1) verifies the environment is not already done (raises RuntimeError if it is), (2) increments the internal step counter, (3) retrieves the current state from state_history and calls the internal _execute_action to produce a new state and an issue code, (4) computes a reward: if issue==0 it uses _calculate_reward(new_state), otherwise applies a PENALTY, appending the reward to reward_history, (5) updates the done flag using _check_termination(new_state), (6) appends the new state and the taken action to state_history and action_history, (7) builds an observation with _get_observation(new_state), and (8) returns a tuple (observation, reward, done, info). The info dict includes step number, issue code, iron_count (count of cells equal to MATERIAL_IDS['iron']), net_force (self.net_force), and the reward. A debug log entry is emitted before returning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_execute_action">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_execute_action</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">In the CcoreFemmEnv class, _execute_action(state, action) applies a discrete movement action to a copy of the provided 2D environment state and returns (new_state, issue_code). It looks up the action's dx/dy from ACTIONS, computes the target grid cell (self.posR+dx, self.posC+dy), and performs checks: if the target is outside self.env_dim it returns the unchanged state with issue_code 3; if the target cell contains MATERIAL_IDS['boundary'] or MATERIAL_IDS['copper'] it returns unchanged state with issue_code 2. On a valid move it updates the agent position (self.posR, self.posC), stamps a 3x3 block of MATERIAL_IDS['iron'] around the new position (clamped to grid borders) to produce a worm-like trail, sets the center cell to MATERIAL_IDS['pointer'], and returns the modified state with issue_code 0 (success). The function works on a copy of the input state so the original is not mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::close">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::close</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method that attempts to shut down the FEMM application and mark the environment closed. It calls femm.closefemm() inside a try/except that silently ignores any exception, then logs an informational message "Environment closed" via self.logger. The method takes no parameters and returns None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_geometry">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_create_geometry</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Initializes and writes a base FEMM geometry for the actuator. The method launches FEMM (femm.openfemm), opens a template file (actuator.fem) from self.femm_path, ensures required materials (Air, Cold rolled low carbon strip steel, Copper) and adds a circuit property named 'icoil'. It then clears existing labels in a 17×35 grid and sets the background block to Air, calls helper routines to create coils, the armature, and boundary geometry (_create_coils, _create_armature, _create_boundaries), zooms to the natural view, saves the result as actuator2.fem, and closes FEMM. The whole operation is wrapped in a try/except that logs and re-raises any exceptions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_calculate_reward">
      <data key="d0">environment::CcoreFemmEnv::_calculate_reward</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of CcoreFemmEnv that computes a scalar reward from a given state by running a FEMM simulation and sampling the magnetic field in the armature. It: opens FEMM and the 'actuator2.fem' document, updates FEMM materials using the provided state (self._update_femm_materials), runs analysis and loads the solution, samples the magnetic flux density B at six specified points (mo_getb at (0.2, 27.5 + arm)), sums the magnitudes, and computes the reward as the average B magnitude multiplied by 1000. The method also selects group 5 to compute a net force via a block integral (integral code 19), stores this in self.net_force, appends it to self.force_history, calls self._calculate_bfield_distribution() to record B-field data, and closes FEMM. On any exception it logs the error and returns a PENALTY value. The method returns the reward as a float and has side effects on the environment state (net_force, force_history and B-field distribution).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f96451a9f4d78491e4b37e23c02a93d5</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">QLearningAgent is a tabular Q-learning agent implementation designed to learn actions in a discrete state/action space (state_size, action_size). It stores Q-values and visit counts in sparse dictionaries (defaultdicts mapping tuple(state) -&amp;gt; np.array of length action_size). Action selection uses an epsilon-greedy policy where exploration samples an action probabilistically according to normalized Q-values (handled by normalize_q_values to avoid degenerate distributions) and exploitation picks argmax Q. The Q-table is updated with the standard Q-learning rule: Q &amp;lt;- Q + learning_rate * (reward + gamma * max_next_Q - Q), and visit counts are incremented. Epsilon is decayed multiplicatively toward epsilon_min using epsilon_decay. Utility methods include get_best_action (pure exploitation), save_q_table/load_q_table (np.savez/np.load with pickled dicts, and restoring epsilon), and get_statistics (returns counts of visited states/actions and current hyperparameters). Configuration parameters (learning_rate, gamma, epsilon settings) are provided via the config dict at initialization; a logger is also created for the class.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::unknown">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">No implementation available to describe. The metadata shows a class QLearningAgent defined in agent.py (line 13), but the specific entity named "unknown" has no associated source code or docstring in the provided data. Therefore its purpose, behavior, parameters, and return values cannot be determined from the given information. To provide a precise description, please supply the actual source code for QLearningAgent or the missing entity implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::decay_epsilon">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::decay_epsilon</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Instance method that reduces the agent's exploration parameter (self.epsilon) by multiplying it by (1 - self.epsilon_decay) while ensuring it never goes below self.epsilon_min. It updates self.epsilon in place to decay exploration over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::choose_action">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::choose_action</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Implements an epsilon-greedy action selector for the QLearningAgent. It first converts the input state to a tuple to look up Q-values in self.q_table. With probability self.epsilon it explores: it obtains the Q-values for that state, converts them to a probability distribution via self.normalize_q_values, and samples an action using np.random.choice(self.action_size, p=probabilities). Otherwise (with probability 1 - self.epsilon) it exploits by returning the index of the maximum Q-value (np.argmax). The method returns the chosen action index (int).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::save_q_table">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::save_q_table</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Saves the agent's Q-learning data to a NumPy .npz file at the given filepath. It writes three items into the archive: 'q_table' (self.q_table converted to a plain dict), 'q_count' (self.q_count converted to a plain dict), and 'epsilon' (the current exploration rate). The function uses numpy.savez and does not return a value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::__init__">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for QLearningAgent that initializes the agent's state and Q-learning parameters. It stores provided state_size, action_size, and config, then reads Q-learning hyperparameters from config['q_learning'] (learning_rate, gamma, epsilon_max used to set initial epsilon, epsilon_min, and epsilon_decay). It creates two defaultdicts for a sparse Q-table (mapping unseen states to zero-filled NumPy arrays of length action_size) and for per-state-action visit counts (q_count). It also initializes simple state-tracking fields (states list and current_episode counter) and a logger for the module.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::normalize_q_values">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::normalize_q_values</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Takes an array of Q-values for the current state and converts them into a probability distribution over actions. If all Q-values are identical (or the max equals the min), it returns a uniform distribution of length self.action_size. Otherwise it linearly rescales values to [0,1] by subtracting the minimum and dividing by the range, then normalizes so the entries sum to 1. The result is a numpy array of probabilities for exploration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::update_q_table">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::update_q_table</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Implements the standard Q-learning update for a single transition (state, action, reward, next_state). It converts state and next_state to tuple keys, looks up the current Q-value for the given state-action and the maximum Q-value for the next state, computes the TD target (reward + gamma * max_next_Q) and TD error, and then updates the stored Q-value by adding learning_rate * TD_error. It also increments the corresponding visit/count entry in self.q_count. The method relies on self.q_table and self.q_count being indexed by tuple(state) and uses self.gamma and self.learning_rate for the update.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::get_best_action">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::get_best_action</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Returns the action index with the highest Q-value for a given state (pure exploitation). The method converts the provided state to a tuple to look up the state's Q-value vector in self.q_table, retrieves that vector, and returns np.argmax(q_values) — the index of the maximum Q-value. If multiple actions tie for the maximum, the first occurrence is returned. (Assumes the state tuple exists as a key in self.q_table.)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::load_q_table">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::load_q_table</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Loads a saved Q-learning state from a NumPy file at the given filepath. The method expects the file to contain keys 'q_table', 'q_count', and 'epsilon'. It loads the file with allow_pickle=True, updates the agent's q_table and q_count dictionaries with the stored values (using dict.update, so existing entries are merged/overwritten), and sets the agent's epsilon to the stored value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="agent::QLearningAgent::get_statistics">
      <data key="d0">agent::QLearningAgent::get_statistics</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Returns a dictionary of simple runtime statistics for the Q-learning agent. It counts total_states_visited as the number of entries in the agent's q_table, computes total_actions_taken by summing the number of action entries stored in self.q_count, and includes the agent's current exploration parameter (current_epsilon), learning_rate, and discount factor (gamma). No parameters; returns a dict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a956404d6cb9375d32037a92e53074b</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">QLearningTrainer encapsulates a Q-learning training workflow for a topology-optimization environment. It builds an agent from the environment-provided agent class, runs the training loop for a configured number of episodes and steps, logs progress, and saves checkpoints and final results. Key responsibilities and behaviors:

- Initialization: stores the environment and configuration, reads episodes and max_steps, computes state and action sizes from the environment, instantiates the agent, and initializes a training history dictionary (episode_rewards, episode_lengths, episode_exploration, best_reward, best_topology).

- train(results_dir): main training loop. For each episode it calls run_episode(), updates history, tracks the best topology (by episode reward), decays the agent epsilon, logs average reward every 10 episodes, and saves a checkpoint every 50 episodes. At the end it saves final results.

- run_episode(): runs a single episode from env.reset() for up to max_steps. Chooses actions using epsilon-greedy (random via env.action_space.sample() when exploring, otherwise agent.choose_action), steps the environment, updates the agent via agent.update_q_table(state, action, reward, next_state), accumulates reward and exploration count, and returns (total_reward, episode_length, exploration_count).

- test_agent(results_dir, num_episodes=5): runs a specified number of test episodes using exploitation only (agent.get_best_action), logs per-episode results, collects final topologies, and saves test_results to test_results.json in the given directory.

- save_checkpoint(results_dir, episode): saves the agent’s Q-table and the history to a checkpoints subdirectory (agent saved as .npz, history as .json).

- save_results(results_dir): writes full training_history.json, final_agent.npz, agent_statistics.json from agent.get_statistics(), and best_topology.json when available.

- get_training_summary(): computes and returns summary statistics (total_episodes, best_reward, average_reward, final_reward, average_length, total_steps, convergence_episode) from the stored history.

Required environment API: get_state_shape(), get_action_size(), get_agent_class(), reset(), step(action), action_space.sample(), get_current_topology(). Required agent API: __init__(state_size,action_size,config), epsilon attribute, decay_epsilon(), choose_action(state), get_best_action(state), update_q_table(state,action,reward,next_state), save_q_table(path), get_statistics(). The class uses numpy, logging, pathlib.Path, tqdm and json for I/O and progress reporting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::unknown">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">QLearningTrainer is a class that encapsulates the training procedure for a Q‑learning agent. Although the source body was not provided, by name and context it implements the high‑level training loop: it manages episodes and timesteps, interacts with the environment to select actions (typically via an epsilon‑greedy policy), receives rewards and next states, and updates the agent’s Q‑estimates (either a Q‑table or a function approximator). It is also responsible for handling common training concerns such as episode/step limits, epsilon (exploration) scheduling/decay, learning rate handling, logging/metrics (episode returns, lengths), and optionally model checkpointing and evaluation. Note: this description is inferred from the class name and typical design; the exact constructor parameters and method names/behaviour cannot be determined because the implementation was not provided.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::run_episode">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::run_episode</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Resets the environment and runs a single Q-learning training episode up to self.max_steps or until the environment signals done. At each step it selects an action with an epsilon-greedy policy (random action with probability self.agent.epsilon, counted as exploration; otherwise uses self.agent.choose_action), executes the action via self.env.step, and updates the agent’s Q-table with self.agent.update_q_table(state, action, reward, next_state). It accumulates total reward, tracks the number of exploration (random) actions, and stops early if done. Returns a tuple (total_reward, episode_length, exploration_count), where episode_length is the number of steps taken.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::save_checkpoint">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::save_checkpoint</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates a "checkpoints" subdirectory under the provided results_dir (if it does not exist), then saves the trainer's current agent Q-table to a file named agent_episode_{episode}.npz using self.agent.save_q_table, and writes the trainer's history dictionary to history_episode_{episode}.json (pretty-printed with indent=2). Parameters: results_dir (Path) and episode (int).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for QLearningTrainer that initializes training state from an environment and configuration. It stores the provided environment and config, reads training parameters episodes and max_steps from config['q_learning'], computes state_size as the product of the environment's state shape and obtains action_size from the environment, and instantiates the agent via the environment-provided agent class with (state_size, action_size, config). It also initializes a training history dictionary (episode_rewards, episode_lengths, episode_exploration, best_reward, best_topology) and sets up a logger for the module.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Runs the Q-Learning training loop for the configured number of episodes. It creates the results directory, logs start information, and then for each episode calls run_episode() to collect episode reward, length, and exploration count. It appends these metrics to history, updates the best_reward and best_topology when a new best reward is seen (via env.get_current_topology()), and decays the agent's exploration rate each episode. The method logs average reward every 10 episodes, saves a checkpoint every 50 episodes (via save_checkpoint), and, after completing all episodes, saves final results (via save_results) and logs completion. A tqdm progress bar is used for episode progress and numpy is used to compute rolling average rewards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::test_agent">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::test_agent</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Runs a fixed number of evaluation episodes with the trained Q‑learning agent (default 5), using exploitation only (agent.get_best_action). For each episode it resets the environment, steps up to self.max_steps or until done, accumulates episode reward and length, and records the environment's final topology. It logs per‑episode reward/length, collects lists under keys 'episode_rewards', 'episode_lengths', and 'final_topologies', writes these results to results_dir/test_results.json, and returns the test_results dictionary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::save_results">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::save_results</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">save_results(self, results_dir) writes the trainer's final outputs to disk under the given Path results_dir. It saves four artifacts: (1) the full training history as JSON to "training_history.json" (self.history), (2) the learned agent Q-table via self.agent.save_q_table(...) to "final_agent.npz" (NumPy .npz), (3) agent statistics from self.agent.get_statistics() as JSON to "agent_statistics.json", and (4) if a best topology is recorded (self.history['best_topology'] is not None), a JSON file "best_topology.json" containing the best topology and its reward. JSON files are written with an indent of 2.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::QLearningTrainer::get_training_summary">
      <data key="d0">trainer::QLearningTrainer::get_training_summary</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Computes and returns summary statistics for training from the trainer's history. If there are no recorded episode rewards it returns an empty dict. Otherwise it reads history['episode_rewards'] and history['episode_lengths'] (converted to numpy arrays) and returns a dict containing: total_episodes (count), best_reward (max reward as float), average_reward (mean reward as float), final_reward (last episode reward as float), average_length (mean episode length as float), total_steps (sum of episode lengths as int), and convergence_episode (1-based index of the first episode with the maximum reward). All numeric values are cast to Python float or int as appropriate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e13cf3187ce7177eba223841947f0cf9</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">AttentionModel is a custom TensorFlow Keras Model that implements an attention-based encoder–decoder built with GRU layers for sequence-to-sequence prediction (returns a per-timestep scalar). Key behaviors:

- Architecture: a small dense preprocessing pipeline (hidden1 -&amp;gt; out) computes an initial decoder/encoder state from static input features (input1). The main sequence input (input2) is processed by an encoder GRU stack that returns the full encoder sequence (encoder_stack_h) and the final encoder state(s).
- Bidirectional option: if bi=True the encoder and decoder GRUs are wrapped as Bidirectional layers and the final encoder states from the two directions are concatenated; otherwise single-direction GRUs are used.
- Decoder: the final encoder state is expanded and repeated across the time dimension to form a decoder input sequence; a GRU decoder produces decoder hidden sequences.
- Attention: a dot-product attention is computed between decoder outputs and encoder outputs (Dot -&amp;gt; softmax) to produce attention weights; the context vector is computed as Dot(attention, encoder_stack_h).
- Output: context and decoder outputs are concatenated and passed through a TimeDistributed Dense(1, activation='sigmoid') layer to produce per-timestep predictions (output shape: [batch, timesteps, 1]).
- Other details: GRU layers use 'elu' activation, the initial state projection uses 'tanh', several BatchNormalization layers are applied to encoder states and context. The model accepts inputs as [input1, input2, mask] and supports masking. get_config/from_config are implemented for (de)serialization.

Notes/limitations: the dropout parameter is stored but not applied in the implementation; masking is forwarded to GRU layers and the final TimeDistributed layer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel::unknown">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">AttentionModel is a tf.keras.Model subclass that implements an attention-based neural network used in the project’s performance-maps prediction pipeline. As a Keras model it is responsible for constructing the model’s layers (encoder/decoder or feature extraction layers and the attention mechanism), performing the forward pass (computing attention weights, producing context vectors and final predictions), and exposing standard model functionality (e.g. call()/predict(), and possibly custom training/evaluation steps or serialization helpers). The exact implementation details (layer types, attention formulation, input/output shapes and any custom methods) are not available in the provided snippet, so this description is based on the class name and its role as a tf.keras.Model in the stated file.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel::__init__">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the AttentionModel class. It stores hyperparameters (bi, input_dim, gru_units, dense_units, dropout) and constructs the Keras layers that make up an encoder–decoder GRU with dot-product attention and preprocessing/postprocessing blocks. Specifically, it creates: a preprocessing Dense (hidden1) and an output Dense (out) with tanh; encoder and decoder GRU layers that are either bidirectional or unidirectional depending on the bi flag (gru_enc_1 returns sequences and state, gru_enc_2 and gru_dec_1 return sequences); BatchNormalization layers (different arrangement for bi vs. uni); attention components implemented as a Dot layer (attention1) followed by softmax (attention2); context computation via another Dot and BatchNormalization (context1/context2); and a TimeDistributed Dense output with sigmoid activation. Note: the dropout value is stored but not applied directly to any layer in this initializer. The method only assembles layer instances and does not perform any forward computation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel::get_config">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel::get_config</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Returns a dictionary of the model’s configuration needed for serialization. The method reads instance attributes and returns a mapping with keys 'bi' (bidirectional flag), 'input_dim', 'gru_units', 'dense_units', and 'dropout'. No parameters; intended to provide the values required to reconstruct or save the AttentionModel.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel::from_config">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel::from_config</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Factory method that constructs and returns a new AttentionModel instance by unpacking the provided config mapping into the class constructor (returns cls(**config)). It expects config to be a dict or mapping of keyword arguments for the class __init__ and performs no additional validation or processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_model::AttentionModel::call">
      <data key="d0">attention_model::AttentionModel::call</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs a forward pass through the attention sequence-to-sequence model. It expects inputs = [input1, input2, mask]. It first projects input1 through self.hidden1 and self.out to produce an initial encoder state. Then it runs an encoder GRU (bi‑ or uni‑directional depending on self.bi) over input2 using that initial state and the supplied mask, and applies batch normalization to the encoder final hidden state(s). The final encoder state is expanded and repeated across the time dimension to form the decoder input; the decoder initial state(s) are set from the encoder final hidden state(s). The decoder GRU produces decoder hidden states, which are combined with encoder hidden stacks through a two-step attention (self.attention1 then self.attention2) to produce attention weights and a context vector (context1 + batch norm). The context is concatenated with decoder outputs and passed through a dense (time‑distributed) layer (respecting mask) to produce the final sequence predictions, which the method returns. The training flag is present in the signature but not otherwise used in this implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5afcd3f028d6f0c0fccb44a062e75800</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">AttentionTrainer is a training utility class for attention-based RNN models implemented with TensorFlow/Keras. It wraps a model and a data loader and implements a complete training workflow: configuration parsing (epochs, learning_rate, batch_size), optimizer (Nadam), loss (MeanSquaredError) and a mean loss metric; a train_step that performs a forward pass, computes MSE loss, backpropagates gradients and updates weights; train_epoch which samples random batches from the provided data_loader, normalizes targets (Y /= 100.0), creates sequence masks for variable-length inputs, performs multiple training iterations per epoch (num_iters = int(num_batches / batch_size) * 2) and accumulates epoch losses; and train which runs the epoch loop, logs scalar loss and layer-weight histograms to TensorBoard, performs qualitative visualization on a sampled batch via data_loader.qualitative_measure, and returns the trained model. It also provides evaluate (computes average test loss over up to 10 batches, using the same normalization and masking), save_model (model.save), and load_model (tf.keras.models.load_model with a custom_objects entry for AttentionModel). The class expects the data_loader to expose names, data_feed(index) -&amp;gt; (X_inp, X, Y, src_len), and qualitative_measure for plotting. It uses numpy and time internally and prints progress and warnings when logging or visualization fail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
