    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::epoch_time">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::epoch_time</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Computes the elapsed time between start_time and end_time (assumed in seconds) and returns it as a tuple of integer minutes and integer seconds. It calculates total elapsed seconds = end_time - start_time, then derives elapsed_mins = int(elapsed_time / 60) and elapsed_secs = int(elapsed_time - (elapsed_mins * 60)).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train_epoch">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train_epoch</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs one training epoch for the AttentionTrainer. It measures start time, computes a number of training iterations from the dataset size and batch_size, and repeatedly samples random batches. For each batch it obtains inputs (X_inp, X), targets (Y) and source lengths from the data_loader, normalizes Y to the 0–1 range and expands its last dimension, builds a boolean mask that marks valid (non-padded) timesteps, and runs a training step via self.train_step. The method collects per-step losses, computes and prints the epoch duration and average loss, and returns the average loss for the epoch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::evaluate">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::evaluate</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Evaluates the trained attention model on up to 10 test batches and returns the average loss. If a test_data_path is provided, it constructs a new data loader of the same class as self.data_loader; otherwise it reuses self.data_loader. For each batch it obtains inputs via test_loader.data_feed(index), scales the ground-truth Y by 1/100 and adds a trailing channel, builds an attention mask from X (masking fully-empty time steps), runs a forward pass self.model([X_inp, X, mask], training=False), computes the loss with self.loss_fn, accumulates and averages the loss across batches, prints the average test loss, and returns that average as a float.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for AttentionTrainer that initializes training state and components. It stores the provided model, data_loader, and config, reads training hyperparameters from config with defaults (epochs=30, learning_rate=0.001, batch_size=32), sets up a Nadam optimizer with the learning rate, uses mean squared error as the loss function, creates a Mean metric to track average loss, and prepares TensorBoard logging by selecting a log directory from config (default 'runs/attention_training') and creating a summary_writer via tf.summary.create_file_writer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Runs the main training loop for the Attention model. For each epoch it calls self.train_epoch(epoch) to perform training and compute the average loss, logs that loss as a scalar to TensorBoard (using self.summary_writer), and attempts to record per-layer weight histograms. After each epoch it also attempts a qualitative evaluation/visualization: it samples a random batch from self.data_loader, normalizes and reshapes the target Y (divides by 100 and expands the last dimension), constructs a padding/mask from X, runs the model in inference mode to obtain predictions, and calls self.data_loader.qualitative_measure(...) to produce visual output. Exceptions during logging or visualization are caught and reported as warnings. After all epochs it flushes TensorBoard summaries, prints completion messages (including the log directory), and returns the trained model. The method relies on instance attributes such as self.epochs, self.batch_size, self.model, self.data_loader, self.summary_writer and self.log_dir.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train_step">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::train_step</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs a single TensorFlow training step for the trainer's model. It runs a forward pass with inputs [X_inp, X, mask] (training=True) inside a tf.GradientTape to obtain predictions, computes the loss via self.loss_fn(Y, y_pred), then computes gradients of that loss with respect to the model's trainable variables and applies them using self.optimizer.apply_gradients. After the update it records the loss into the metric self.mean_loss and returns the loss tensor and the model predictions (y_pred).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::save_model">
      <data key="d0">attention_trainer::AttentionTrainer::save_model</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of attention_trainer.AttentionTrainer that saves the trainer's model to disk. It calls self.model.save(filepath) using the provided filepath (string) and prints a confirmation message showing where the model was saved. The method does not return a value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-968bf1bdb0ca466dad9c6091ddfd8491</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
