    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::data_feed">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::data_feed</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">data_feed(self, index) builds and returns one batch of sequential training/inference data starting at the given index. It uses internal attributes (batch_size, seq_len_sorted, seq_len_order, inp_dim, dim_x, dim_y, text_path, hdf5_path) to: 1) determine the maximum sequence length for the batch and allocate a zero-padded tensor b of shape (batch_size, max_seq_len, inp_dim) and an input tensor X_inp of shape (batch_size, dim_x, dim_y); 2) for each item in the batch, read a corresponding text file (named '{file_number+1}.txt'), skip the first two header lines, parse the remaining rows to floats, clamp negative values to 0, and copy the parsed sequence into b up to its file-specific length (src_len); 3) for the same file index, read the HDF5 dataset '/DS_TrINP', skip its first row, and store that array into X_inp; 4) after the loop, split b into X (all feature columns except the last) and Y (the last column as the target), and return the tuple (X_inp, X, Y, src_len). Returned shapes: X_inp: (batch_size, dim_x, dim_y), X: (batch_size, max_seq_len, inp_dim-1), Y: (batch_size, max_seq_len), and src_len: list of sequence lengths for each batch element.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::qualitative_measure">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::qualitative_measure</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">A DataLoader method that prepares and sends data to a MATLAB visualization routine to produce a qualitative performance map. It takes arrays of speed (Nm), torque (T), target values (Tar), predicted values (Pred) and a local index, looks up a file_number using the instance attributes seq_len_order and batch_size, creates an index array of the same size, stacks Nm, T, Tar, Pred and the index into a single array, converts that stack to a matlab.double, and calls the MATLAB function drawEffMap via the MATLAB engine stored in self.eng. If the MATLAB engine is not available (self.eng is None) it prints a message and returns without plotting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::__init__">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the DataLoader class. It accepts a data_path (converted to a pathlib.Path) and optional parameters data_type (default 'efficiency'), batch_size (2), dim_x (14), and dim_y (1). It stores these settings, determines the text and HDF5 file paths based on data_type (supports 'efficiency' and 'powerfactor' only, otherwise raises ValueError), and verifies that these paths exist (raising FileNotFoundError if not). It initializes several internal attributes (names list, inp_dim=0, seq_len, seq_len_sorted, seq_len_order) and sets an evaluation efficiency threshold (eff_threshold=85). It then calls self.get_sequence_len() to populate sequence-length information. Finally, it attempts to start a MATLAB engine and add the data path to MATLAB's path; if MATLAB cannot be started it prints a warning and sets self.eng to None.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::quantitative_measure">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::quantitative_measure</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Converts continuous target (Tar) and predicted (Pred) arrays into binary labels by thresholding at self.eff_threshold (values &amp;gt; threshold → 1, else 0), computes the binary confusion matrix, saves the four counts (TN FP FN TP) to a text file named using self.seq_len_order[index + self.batch_size] inside self.data_path, and returns the confusion matrix array. The method relies on self.eff_threshold, self.seq_len_order, self.batch_size, and self.data_path and writes a header line 'TN FP FN TP' followed by the counts separated by spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::get_sequence_len">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::get_sequence_len</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Reads all .txt files in self.text_path (matching "*.txt") and, for each file named as a 1-based integer (e.g. 1.txt, 2.txt, ...), opens the file and parses the first line as integers. It appends the first integer from that line to self.seq_len (interpreted as the sequence length), appends the file number to self.names, and sets self.inp_dim to the second integer (input dimension) from the most recently read file. After processing all files it computes two orderings: self.seq_len_order (the list of indices sorted by sequence length) and self.seq_len_sorted (the sorted sequence lengths). The method returns nothing and relies on the assumption that each file’s first line contains at least two integers and files are numerically named.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data_loader::DataLoader::get_data_info">
      <data key="d0">data_loader::DataLoader::get_data_info</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the DataLoader class that returns a summary dictionary about the currently loaded dataset. It includes: the data type (self.data_type); number of files (len(self.names)); input dimension (self.inp_dim); sequence length statistics (min, max and mean computed from self.seq_len using numpy); and the data path converted to a string (str(self.data_path)). Assumes the instance has attributes names, inp_dim, seq_len and data_path.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e758f46de7980f7b381133c2079910f4</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_model::RNNModel">
      <data key="d0">rnn_model::RNNModel</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">RNNModel is a TensorFlow Keras Model that implements a small recurrent network for per-timestep prediction (e.g., efficiency/power factor). Constructor arguments control whether the GRUs are bidirectional (bi), the dimensionality of the dense preprocessing (input_dim), the number of GRU units (gru_units) and the size of an intermediate dense layer (dense_units).

Architecture and behavior:
- Two dense preprocessing layers (hidden1 -&amp;gt; out) transform a non-sequential input (input1) into an initial RNN state vector of size gru_units.
- A first GRU layer (gru_1) consumes a sequential input (input2) and uses the transformed dense output as its initial state. If bi=True this layer is Bidirectional and returns sequence outputs plus states; otherwise it is a single-direction GRU returning sequence and a single state.
- A second GRU layer (gru_2) processes the sequence output from the first GRU; its unit count is gru_units/2.
- A TimeDistributed Dense with a sigmoid activation produces a scalar prediction per timestep, so the final output shape is (batch, timesteps, 1).
- A mask argument is passed through to GRU layers and the final dense to support variable-length sequences.

Serialization helpers:
- get_config returns the constructor settings (bi, input_dim, gru_units, dense_units) and from_config reconstructs the model from that config.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70b701613085407db97b5deace4233ed</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_model::RNNModel::unknown">
      <data key="d0">rnn_model::RNNModel::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">This entity is the class declaration for RNNModel which subclasses tf.keras.Model. The provided metadata shows only the class header (class RNNModel(tf.keras.Model):) and the actual implementation body is not available in the supplied snippet. In typical usage this class would encapsulate an RNN-based neural network for the project’s performance-maps prediction task — i.e. it would define the model architecture (recurrent layers such as LSTM/GRU, any dense/output layers), implement the forward pass (call / call_once / __call__), and hold configuration and helper methods for training, evaluation and inference. Because the implementation is missing from the snippet, the precise layer types, input/output shapes, and any custom behavior or hyperparameters cannot be determined here; to see exact behavior inspect the full rnn_model.py file (the class body below the shown declaration).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70b701613085407db97b5deace4233ed</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
