    <node id="rnn_model::RNNModel::get_config">
      <data key="d0">rnn_model::RNNModel::get_config</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Instance method that returns a dictionary of the RNNModel's configuration needed for serialization/reconstruction. It packages the model's key attributes — 'bi' (bidirectional flag), 'input_dim', 'gru_units', and 'dense_units' — into a dict and returns it. No parameters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70b701613085407db97b5deace4233ed</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_model::RNNModel::call">
      <data key="d0">rnn_model::RNNModel::call</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs the model's forward pass. Expects inputs = [input1, input2, mask] where input1 is a static/feature vector and input2 is a sequence (mask is an optional sequence mask). It computes a hidden representation h = out(hidden1(input1)) and uses h as the initial state(s) for the first GRU layer. If self.bi is True it provides a pair [h,h] as initial_state (and expects the GRU to return (x, state_h, state_c)); otherwise it provides h as the single initial state (and expects (x, state_h)). The sequence output x is then passed through a second GRU (with the same mask) and a final dense layer to produce the model predictions. Note: the training argument is accepted but not used in this implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70b701613085407db97b5deace4233ed</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">RNNTrainer is a convenience training/evaluation wrapper for a TensorFlow Keras RNN model. It encapsulates the training loop, optimization, logging and model persistence. Key behaviors:
- Initialization: stores model, data_loader and config; reads epochs, learning_rate and batch_size (defaults provided); constructs a Nadam optimizer, MSE loss, a tf.metrics.Mean for tracking loss and a TensorBoard summary writer.
- train_step: runs a single forward/backward pass using tf.GradientTape, computes MSE loss, applies gradients to model.trainable_variables and updates the running mean_loss metric. Expects the model to accept [X_inp, X, mask] as input.
- train_epoch: runs many training iterations in an epoch by sampling random batches from data_loader.data_feed(index). It normalizes target Y by dividing by 100 and expands its last axis, builds a mask for variable-length sequences from X, and collects per-step losses to print and return the epoch-average loss.
- train: main loop invoking train_epoch for configured epochs, writes loss scalars and (attempts to) log per-layer weight histograms to TensorBoard, produces a qualitative visualization each epoch via data_loader.qualitative_measure, flushes summaries and returns the trained model.
- evaluate: runs the model on up to 10 batches from either the provided test_data_path (instantiating the same data_loader class) or the training data_loader, computes average MSE loss and prints it.
- save_model / load_model: wrapper helpers to save and load the Keras model. load_model attempts to pass custom_objects={'RNNModel': RNNModel} when loading.

Assumptions / expected data_loader API: data_loader.names length is used to determine dataset size; data_loader.data_feed(index) returns (X_inp, X, Y, src_len); data_loader.data_type and qualitative_measure exist for visualization and re-instantiation.

Important implementation notes / caveats:
- Targets are scaled by 1/100 and expanded in axis before loss computation.
- The mask construction (np.logical_not on X followed by np.logical_not(np.all(..., axis=-1))) produces a boolean mask indicating non-empty sequence rows; the model must accept that mask shape.
- num_iters is computed as int(num_batches / batch_size) * 2 which may not match dataset size and will produce a seemingly arbitrary number of iterations per epoch.
- There is a formatting/syntax bug in evaluate's print statement and load_model references RNNModel which must be in scope; TensorBoard weight logging is wrapped in a try/except to avoid crashes if layers are not loggable.

In short, RNNTrainer provides a simple, configurable training/evaluation pipeline with TensorBoard support for sequence models but relies on a specific data_loader interface and contains a couple of implementation quirks that may require fixing for production use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
