    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train_epoch">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train_epoch</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs one training epoch for the RNN model. It measures start/end time, computes a number of random minibatch iterations (num_iters = 2 * floor(num_batches / batch_size)), and for each iteration samples a random batch from the data loader via data_feed. For each batch it scales the targets Y by 1/100 and expands their last dimension, builds a boolean mask that identifies valid (non-padded) timesteps from X, and calls self.train_step(X_inp, X, Y, mask) to perform a training update and obtain the loss and predictions. It collects per-iteration losses, prints the epoch number, elapsed time and average loss, and returns the average loss for the epoch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::epoch_time">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::epoch_time</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method that computes the elapsed time between two timestamps (start_time and end_time) and returns it as a tuple of whole minutes and whole seconds. It subtracts start_time from end_time to get elapsed seconds, converts that to minutes via integer division (int(elapsed_time / 60)) and computes remaining seconds as int(elapsed_time - minutes * 60). Both returned values are integers; fractional seconds are truncated rather than rounded.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::evaluate">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::evaluate</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Evaluates the trained RNN by running it on up to 10 mini-batches from a test dataset (either the trainer's existing data_loader or a new one constructed from test_data_path). For each batch it: obtains inputs via data_feed, scales the targets Y by 1/100 and expands their last dimension, builds a timestep mask from X to indicate valid positions, runs the model in inference mode (training=False) with [X_inp, X, mask], computes the loss with self.loss_fn, and accumulates it. After iterating the selected batches it computes and prints the average test loss and returns that average (float). Note: the number of tested batches is min(10, len(test_loader.names) - self.batch_size).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the RNNTrainer class that initializes training state and components. It stores the provided model, data_loader, and config; reads training hyperparameters from config with defaults (epochs=50, learning_rate=0.001, batch_size=32); creates a Nadam optimizer with the learning rate; sets the loss function to mean squared error; creates a Mean metric to track average loss; and configures a TensorBoard summary writer using log_dir from config (default 'runs/rnn_training').</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Main training loop for the RNN model. When called it: prints startup information, iterates for self.epochs epochs and for each epoch calls self.train_epoch(epoch) to perform training and obtain the average loss; logs that loss to TensorBoard (self.summary_writer) and attempts to log model layer weights as histograms (printing a warning if weight logging fails); performs a qualitative/visual evaluation by selecting a random sample batch from self.data_loader (using a random index), prepares inputs (X_inp, X, Y, src_len), scales Y by 1/100 and expands its channel dimension, constructs a mask from X, runs the model in inference mode to get predictions and converts them to numpy, and calls self.data_loader.qualitative_measure to generate a visualization (printing a warning if visualization fails). After all epochs it flushes TensorBoard summaries, prints completion messages (including the log directory), and returns the trained model (self.model). The method relies on attributes such as self.epochs, self.summary_writer, self.batch_size, self.data_loader, self.model, and self.log_dir and handles errors during logging/visualization by printing warnings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train_step">
      <data key="d0">rnn_trainer::RNNTrainer::train_step</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs a single training step for the RNNTrainer instance. Given input tensors (X_inp, X, mask) and targets Y, it runs a forward pass through self.model with training=True to produce predictions, computes the loss via self.loss_fn(Y, y_pred) inside a tf.GradientTape, computes gradients w.r.t. the model's trainable variables and applies them with self.optimizer.apply_gradients. It also updates the training loss metric self.mean_loss with the current loss and returns the loss value and the model predictions (loss, y_pred).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fedc9175cfabc4ec85199edd5a871275</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
