    <node id="model::get_model">
      <data key="d0">model::get_model</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">get_model(img_size=(400, 400)) constructs and returns a Keras convolutional neural network intended to predict a single-channel field map from a single-channel image input. The network accepts an input tensor of shape img_size + (1,), applies an initial BatchNormalization and then a stack of convolutional blocks (Conv2D -&amp;gt; BatchNormalization -&amp;gt; Dropout) increasing channel depth (64 → 128). The decoder path uses Conv2DTranspose layers with BatchNormalization, Dropout and additive skip/residual connections that merge intermediate encoder activations back into the corresponding decoder stages (U‑Net / residual style), while all convolutions use padding='same' and ReLU activations. The final layer is a 1×1 Conv2D producing a single-channel output with ReLU activation (so outputs are non-negative). The function returns the assembled keras.Model. Note: the architecture does not use explicit pooling or strided convolutions; heavy dropout (0.5) is applied throughout.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7da282e8b642ab09f2eb1cc5bf6354be</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="model::unknown">
      <data key="d0">model::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">A factory function named get_model that accepts an optional img_size parameter (default (400, 400)). It is intended to construct and return a deep-learning model appropriate for input images of the given spatial size. The implementation body is not shown in the provided snippet, so the exact architecture, framework (e.g., Keras/PyTorch), and whether the model is compiled/configured are not available. In typical usage you call get_model(img_size=(width, height)) to obtain a model instance ready for training or inference in the MagneticFieldPredictor project.</data>
