    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">DLTrainer is a small training utility class that prepares data and runs a custom TensorFlow training loop for a magnetic-field prediction model. Key behaviors:

- Initialization/load_config: loads configuration from a YAML file (config_dl.yaml by default) or falls back to a built-in default config (model image size, training hyperparameters, data normalization).

- get_B_stats(files, folder_path): iterates CSV files (expected format columns: x,y,B,mat) and computes the minimum and maximum B-field values across the dataset.

- generate_geom_array(ds, cr, cm): constructs a 400×400 geometry input array with a circular mask (radius computed from domain size and coil radius) filled with the given current magnitude; returns the array.

- data_feed(index, files, folder_path, batch_size): builds a batch of inputs X and targets y by:
  - parsing coil_radius, domain_size, and current_magnitude from each filename,
  - reading the CSV into a DataFrame,
  - generating the geometry input with generate_geom_array,
  - gridding scattered B values onto a 400×400 grid using scipy.interpolate.griddata,
  - returning X (geometry), y_norm (targets scaled by 1000), and the original y.

- my_training(batch_size, epochs, no_iters, lr): sets up a data folder ('data/raw/NL_Data2'), lists CSV files, computes B_min/B_max, stores normalization denominator, creates the model via get_model(img_size=(400,400)), constructs a Nadam optimizer, and runs a custom training loop using tf.GradientTape and mean-squared-error loss. Returns the trained model.

- train(results_dir): top-level routine that reads config hyperparameters, computes iterations, calls my_training, saves the model to results_dir/model/field_predictor_model.h5 and writes a training_metrics.json file.

Notes and implementation details/limitations:
- The code depends on many external names (pandas as pd, numpy as np, Path, glob, time, scipy.interpolate.griddata, tensorflow as tf, get_model) being imported elsewhere or globally.
- There is a likely bug: train() computes no_iters using self.data_size before self.data_size is initialized (it is set inside my_training), which will raise an AttributeError or produce incorrect behavior.
- Some calls (e.g., glob.glob1) expect string directory paths while the code passes Path objects; careful import/usage is required.
- The training loop computes gradients from per-sample losses without reducing them explicitly (uses tf.keras.losses.mean_squared_error which returns element-wise values); this may produce shape/aggregation issues unless TensorFlow reduces the loss later.

Overall, DLTrainer glues together data loading/preprocessing (from CSVs and filenames), geometry input generation, model training, and model/metrics saving for a magnetic-field prediction task.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
