    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::get_B_stats">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::get_B_stats</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method get_B_stats(self, files, folder_path) reads a list of CSV files (using pandas.read_csv with header=None) located under folder_path, renames the columns to ['x','y','B','mat'], and computes the minimum and maximum values of the 'B' column across all provided files. It initializes running extremes with {'max': 0, 'min': 1}, updates them while iterating over files, and returns a tuple (min_B, max_B). The folder_path is used with the / operator (so typically a pathlib.Path), and files is an iterable of filenames.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::data_feed">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::data_feed</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the DLTrainer class that builds a training batch of input geometries and target magnetic field maps from CSV files. It adjusts the start index to avoid overrunning the file list, selects batch_size filenames, and allocates X and y arrays of shape (batch_size, 400, 400, 1). For each file it: parses coil_radius, domain_size and current_magnitude from the filename; reads the CSV (assumed columns x, y, B, mat) into a DataFrame; calls self.generate_geom_array(domain_size, coil_radius, current_magnitude) to produce the input geometry array; and interpolates the scattered B values onto a regular 400x400 grid over [-domain_size, domain_size] using scipy.interpolate.griddata (linear). The geometry array is stored in X and the interpolated B field (transposed) is stored in y. The method returns three arrays: X (inputs), y_norm (y scaled by 1000), and y (original interpolated targets). Note: the code assumes a specific filename format and does not handle griddata NaNs or file-format errors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for trainer.DLTrainer. It accepts an optional config_path (defaults to None), stores it on self as self.config_path, and immediately calls self.load_config() to load the trainer configuration. Aside from setting that attribute and invoking load_config, it performs no other initialization or validation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::load_config">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::load_config</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Loads the trainer configuration into self.config. It chooses a config file path: uses self.config_path if set, otherwise 'config_dl.yaml'. If that file exists, it reads and parses it with yaml.safe_load; if not, it assigns a built-in default configuration dictionary (model image size and channels, training batch_size/epochs/learning_rate, and data normalization). The function imports yaml locally and stores the result on self.config.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::generate_geom_array">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::generate_geom_array</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Creates a 400×400 NumPy array (dtype float) initialized to zeros and draws a filled circle centered in the array. The circle radius is computed as r = (cr * 200) / ds, and every pixel whose distance from the array center ((200,200)) is strictly less than r is set to the value cm. The function returns this array (so the result is a circular patch of value cm on a zero background).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::my_training">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::my_training</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">A custom training loop method of the DLTrainer class that builds and trains a Keras model from raw CSV data. Given batch_size, epochs, no_iters and lr, it: 1) locates CSV files under ./data/raw/NL_Data2 and stores the dataset size in self.data_size; 2) computes B_min and B_max via self.get_B_stats(files, folder_path) and stores a normalization denominator in self.norm_denom; 3) creates a model with get_model(img_size=(400,400)) and configures a Nadam optimizer with the provided learning rate; 4) for each epoch runs no_iters training iterations where it samples a random start index, obtains a batch via self.data_feed(index, files, folder_path, batch_size), computes predictions, computes mean squared error loss, computes gradients with tf.GradientTape and applies them to model.trainable_variables; 5) prints epoch timing and returns the trained tf.keras.Model. Side effects: sets self.data_size and self.norm_denom and relies on external methods (get_B_stats, data_feed) and get_model. Note: there is no validation, checkpointing or explicit loss reduction in this implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::DLTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">trainer::DLTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">This method is the main training entry point for DLTrainer. It reads training settings (batch_size, epochs, learning_rate) from self.config['training'], computes a heuristic number of iterations no_iters = 2 * floor(data_size / batch_size), and calls self.my_training(batch_size, epochs, no_iters, lr) to perform the actual training and obtain a trained model. After training it creates a "model" subdirectory under the provided results_dir, saves the Keras model as field_predictor_model.h5, and writes a JSON file training_metrics.json containing the batch size, epochs, learning rate, dataset size and a placeholder final_loss ('N/A'). The function prints progress messages but does not return a value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17571f07209a721c1eaf816fa7ab2e50</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">PINNTrainer is a simple trainer class for a physics‑informed XPINN model. It loads training configuration from a YAML file (or falls back to a hard‑coded default), generates random training data (domain bounds, boundary points, collocation points for two subdomains, and interface points at y=0), instantiates an external XPINN model, runs a synchronous training loop that calls model.train_step() for a configured number of iterations while logging losses periodically, and finally saves model weights, training metrics, and test predictions as JSON files into a results directory. The trainer expects the XPINN object to provide setup_training(...), train_step(), get_loss(), predict(...), and TensorFlow variables accessible via model.sess for evaluation. Notes: the data generation uses a fixed domain [-1,1], interface solution defaults to zeros, and the implementation overwrites the earlier 2D upper‑bound vector (ub) with a different array when building boundary labels which can cause unexpected behavior in X_f1/X_f2 generation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer::unknown">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to an entity named "unknown" inside the class PINNTrainer, but no implementation, signature, or docstring is provided in the supplied source metadata. There is no code to inspect for behavior, inputs, or outputs, so its purpose cannot be determined from the available information. Likely this is a missing / placeholder member or an artifact of an incomplete extraction; inspect the actual file around line 14 in trainer.py to see the real implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer::__init__">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for trainer::PINNTrainer. It accepts an optional config_path (default None), saves it to the instance as self.config_path, and immediately calls self.load_config() to load the trainer configuration. No other initialization is performed in this method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer::load_config">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer::load_config</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Reads a PINN trainer configuration into the instance. The method selects a YAML file named by self.config_path (or 'config_pinn.yaml' if self.config_path is not set), attempts to open and parse it with yaml.safe_load, and stores the resulting mapping in self.config. If the file does not exist, it populates self.config with a built-in default dictionary containing sections for 'xpinn' (network layer sizes and scaling/multiplier parameters), 'training' (max iterations, Adam learning rate, and sample sizes), and 'physics' (equation and domain_decomposition). The yaml module is imported locally in the method.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer::train">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer::train</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Trains an XPINN (physics-informed neural network) and saves results. The method reads XPINN and training hyperparameters from self.config, generates training data via generate_training_data(), constructs an XPINN model (XPINN(layers1, layers2, mu1, mu2)), and calls model.setup_training(...) with the generated data. It runs a training loop for config['training']['max_iter'] iterations, performing model.train_step() each iteration and recording/printing losses every 1000 iterations (using model.get_loss()). After training it records total elapsed time, creates a results_dir/model folder and writes the trained network parameters (weights1, biases1, A1, weights2, biases2, A2) to xpinn_weights.json by evaluating TensorFlow variables from model.sess, saves training metadata and loss history to training_metrics.json, generates random test inputs, obtains predictions via model.predict(...), and writes those predictions to test_predictions.json. The function prints progress and final locations of saved results.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="trainer::PINNTrainer::generate_training_data">
      <data key="d0">trainer::PINNTrainer::generate_training_data</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">generate_training_data constructs a random training dataset for an XPINN over a 2D domain. It sets the domain bounds to [-1, 1] × [-1, 1] and draws uniformly random points for: boundary points X_ub (N_ub × 2) with N_ub from config and associated boundary values ub set to zeros (N_ub × 1); collocation points for subdomain 1 (X_f1, N_f1 × 2) and subdomain 2 (X_f2, N_f2 × 2) using counts from the config; and interface points X_i1 (N_i1 × 2) placed on the line y = 0 with x sampled uniformly (np.random.rand) and corresponding interface solution u_i1 set to zeros (N_i1 × 1). All spatial samples are drawn with numpy randomness. Note: the implementation overwrites the earlier variable ub (the upper bound) with a zeros array before computing X_f1 and X_f2, and samples X_i1.x in [0,1), so the code likely does not behave exactly as intended and can produce broadcasting errors or unintended sampling ranges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0eac402b8ebc2961b7a934e328f3ac6c</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">XPINN is an implementation of an extended physics‑informed neural network (XPINN) built with TensorFlow 1.x that decomposes the domain into two sub‑networks. The class initializes two feed‑forward neural networks (weights, biases and a per‑layer learnable scalar A) using Xavier weight initialization and tanh activations that are scaled by a constant scaling_factor and the learnable A parameters. The two subnets (net_u1, net_u2) accept concatenated (x,y) inputs. The net_f routine computes first and second derivatives (u_x, u_y, u_xx, u_yy) for both subnets and at interface points via tf.gradients, applies a nonlinear gradient‑dependent scaling c (computed from squared gradient magnitudes), and builds PDE residuals f1, f2 and the interface residual fi1 plus the interface average and individual interface predictions. setup_training creates placeholders, wires the prediction and residual tensors into two loss functions (loss1 for subdomain1 and loss2 for subdomain2) that combine boundary data, PDE residuals, interface continuity and average‑solution constraints, and sets up two Adam minimization ops (one per loss). train_step runs the two Adam train ops, predict evaluates subnet predictions for given test points, and get_loss returns the current loss values. The implementation uses double precision (tf.float64), tf.Session and placeholders (TensorFlow 1.x API), and exposes hyperparameters multiplier and scaling_factor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::unknown">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::unknown</data>
      <data key="d1">classs</data>
      <data key="d2">No implementation was provided for xpinn_model::XPINN::unknown (only the class declaration "class XPINN:" was shown), so the exact behavior cannot be determined from the supplied snippet. Based on the common meaning of an "XPINN" in physics‑informed neural network code, this class is typically the top‑level model that implements an eXtended/Domain‑Decomposed PINN. In practice such a class usually: (1) constructs and holds one or more subdomain neural networks (sub‑PINNs), (2) assembles the overall loss including PDE residuals in each subdomain, boundary condition terms, and interface continuity/flux penalties between subdomains, (3) provides forward/prediction methods to evaluate the solution at given points, (4) implements training utilities (optimizers, batching, training loop, loss logging, checkpoint save/load), and (5) exposes configuration parameters for network architectures, collocation points, and physical constants. If you can provide the class body, I can give a precise description of its actual implementation and methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::net_u1">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::net_u1</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method net_u1 of the XPINN class computes the prediction of the first sub-network (subdomain 1). It concatenates input tensors x and y along axis 1, then passes the combined input through the class neural_net_tanh function using the network parameters weights1, biases1 and A1, and returns the resulting output tensor u (the subdomain-1 network prediction).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::xavier_init">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::xavier_init</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the XPINN class that creates a weight matrix initialized with Xavier/Glorot initialization. It expects a two-element shape/size (size[0] = in_dim, size[1] = out_dim), computes the Xavier standard deviation as sqrt(2/(in_dim + out_dim)), samples values from a truncated normal distribution with that stddev and shape [in_dim, out_dim], casts the samples to double, and returns them as a TensorFlow Variable with dtype=tf.float64.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::net_f">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::net_f</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">net_f computes physics-informed quantities (PDE residuals and interface terms) for a two-subdomain XPINN. Given coordinates for subdomain-1 (x1,y1), subdomain-2 (x2,y2) and an interface (xi1,yi1), it: • evaluates the two subnetworks u1 and u2 and their first and second derivatives via TensorFlow gradients; • for subdomain-1 (and its interface point) it computes a nonlinear coefficient c = 1/(1 + 0.05|∇u1|^2) (intermediate d1 is computed but unused) and scales the first derivatives by c before taking second derivatives; • for subdomain-2 it uses raw derivatives (no scaling) to compute second derivatives; • forms PDE residuals f1 and f2 as Laplacian(u) minus given source terms (exp(x)+exp(y) and -1+exp(x)+exp(y) respectively); • forms an interface residual fi1 as the difference between subdomain-1 and subdomain-2 residuals at the interface; • returns (f1, f2, fi1), the average solution on the interface uavgi1 = (u1i1+u2i1)/2, and the two interface network values u1i1 and u2i1. These outputs are intended for use in the physics-informed loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::train_step">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::train_step</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Performs a single TensorFlow 1.x training step for the XPINN model. It builds a feed dictionary that maps the model placeholders (x1_ub_tf, y1_ub_tf, x_f1_tf, y_f1_tf, x_f2_tf, y_f2_tf, x_fi1_tf, y_fi1_tf) to the corresponding NumPy arrays stored on self (x1_ub, y1_ub, x_f1, y_f1, x_f2, y_f2, x_fi1, y_fi1), then runs two optimizer update operations (train_op_Adam1 and train_op_Adam2) in the session (self.sess). The method does not return a value.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::__init__">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::__init__</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Constructor for the XPINN class. It records the provided network architectures (layers1, layers2) and physical/hyper-parameters (mu1 and mu2, defaulting to 1), sets two scalar attributes multiplier and scaling_factor to 20, initializes two neural sub-networks by calling self.initialize_NN for each layer list and stores their returned weights, biases and A matrices as weights1/biases1/A1 and weights2/biases2/A2, and creates a TensorFlow 1.x session (self.sess = tf.Session()).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::neural_net_tanh">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::neural_net_tanh</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the XPINN class that builds a feedforward neural network using tanh activations on the hidden layers and a linear output layer. It takes an input tensor X, lists of weight and bias tensors, and a per-layer scaling list A. It iteratively applies affine transforms H = H @ W + b for each hidden layer, multiplies the pre-activation by self.scaling_factor * A[l], applies tf.tanh, and then applies a final linear layer (no activation) to produce the output Y. Weights, biases and A must have shapes compatible with the successive layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::initialize_NN">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::initialize_NN</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">initialize_NN(self, layers) builds and returns the trainable parameters for a feed‑forward neural network given a list of layer sizes. For each pair of consecutive layers it: (1) creates a weight matrix with Xavier initialization of shape [layers[l], layers[l+1]] via self.xavier_init, (2) creates a bias tf.Variable of zeros with shape [1, layers[l+1]] (dtype=tf.float64), and (3) creates a trainable scalar tf.Variable a initialized to 0.05 (dtype=tf.float64). It repeats this for every adjacent layer pair (num_layers-1 times) and returns three lists: weights, biases, and A (the per-layer scalar variables).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::net_u2">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::net_u2</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the XPINN class that computes the predicted field u for subdomain 2. It concatenates the input tensors x and y along axis 1, feeds the result through the instance's neural_net_tanh using the parameters weights2, biases2 and A2, and returns the network output u.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::setup_training">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::setup_training</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Initializes the training setup for the XPINN model: it unpacks the provided training arrays into per-domain x/y components and stores boundary/interface data on the instance. It creates TensorFlow 1.x placeholders for the boundary, collocation and interface inputs, calls the model forward passes (net_u1, net_u2, net_f) to get predictions and PDE residuals, and then builds two loss functions (loss1 and loss2). The losses combine boundary-condition terms, PDE residual terms, interface consistency terms (including u_fi, u1i1/u2i1, and their average uavgi1) and a user-scaled multiplier. It sets up an Adam optimizer (learning rate 0.0008) with separate minimize operations for each loss, then initializes all TF variables via the session. The method therefore wires inputs, losses, optimizers and runs the TF global variable initializer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::predict">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::predict</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method predict(X_star1, X_star2) runs the model's TensorFlow session to compute and return predicted u-values for two subdomains. It expects X_star1 and X_star2 to be arrays where column 0 is x and column 1 is y; it feeds X_star1[:,0:1] and X_star1[:,1:2] into the placeholders x1_ub_tf and y1_ub_tf to evaluate ub1_pred, and feeds X_star2[:,0:1] and X_star2[:,1:2] into x_f2_tf and y_f2_tf to evaluate ub2_pred. The method returns the two prediction arrays (u_star1, u_star2).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="xpinn_model::XPINN::get_loss">
      <data key="d0">xpinn_model::XPINN::get_loss</data>
      <data key="d1">functions</data>
      <data key="d2">Method of the XPINN class that returns the current numeric values of the model losses. It builds a TensorFlow feed dictionary by mapping the model's TF placeholders (x1_ub_tf, y1_ub_tf, x_f1_tf, y_f1_tf, x_f2_tf, y_f2_tf, x_fi1_tf, y_fi1_tf) to the corresponding stored data arrays on the instance (x1_ub, y1_ub, x_f1, y_f1, x_f2, y_f2, x_fi1, y_fi1), runs self.sess.run to evaluate self.loss1 and self.loss2, and returns the two loss values (loss1_value, loss2_value). No arguments are required.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb67c00596095055d02744abf9e00f74</data>
      <data key="d4">custom_kg</data>
      <data key="d5">1759701943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="main">
      <data key="d0">main</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9faa1c94bade9df8eda3dfcb844c497b</data>
